Your Mastra agents can now receive Ring doorbell events using[Ring signals](https://mastra.ai/integrations/channels/ring) .


Each Ring event arrives in the agent's thread as a[notification signal](https://mastra.ai/docs/signals/notifications) with a priority you can configure. The agent can then fetch a snapshot or video clip from the Ring API and decide what to do when there's someone at your door.


Ring signals turn a doorbell into an event source agents can act on. A home security agent could watch for` human` motion, view the snapshot, and flag visits that might need urgent attention. A delivery assistant could use` package_delivery` motion to let you know a parcel has arrived, or a driveway agent could log` vehicle` motion to track which cars pull up.


Your browser does not support the video tag.A Ring motion_detected clip plays in the agent's thread as a person in a balaclava walks up to the front door, then a button_press notification shows them at the camera and the agent replies that the visit needs attention.


Agents are usually configured to respond to software events, like messages from Slack, Discord, iMessage, and other supported[channel providers](https://mastra.ai/integrations#channels) . Ring signals extend that functionality, making it possible to interact with the **real world** . With[memory](https://mastra.ai/docs/memory/observational-memory) enabled, you can teach your agent about what it's seen, telling it which people, vehicles, and activity to recognize, so it remembers them for future events.


By default, the agent receives a snapshot image of each event. Set` visibleToModel: true` on` tools.clip` and use a model capable of receiving video input, and the agent can watch the videos too.


## Get started


Ring signals need a Ring developer app linked to your Ring account. Follow the setup in the[Ring integration guide](https://mastra.ai/integrations/channels/ring#prerequisites) , then install the following dependencies:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/ring-signals   @mastra/libsql   @mastra/memory
```


note


Requires` @mastra/ring-signals@0.1.0` and` @mastra/core@1.81.0` or later, added in[PR #28864](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/pull/28864) .


Add` RingSignals` to your agent's` signals` :


src/mastra/agents/home-security-agent.ts


```text
import   {   Agent   }   from   "  @mastra/core/agent  "  ;
import   {   Memory   }   from   "  @mastra/memory  "  ;
import   {   RingSignals   }   from   "  @mastra/ring-signals  "  ;


export   const   homeSecurityAgent   =   new   Agent  ({
id:   "  home-security-agent  "  ,
name:   "  Home Security Agent  "  ,
instructions:   "  Watch the front door and say briefly whether each event needs attention.  "  ,
model:   "  google/gemini-3.8-flash  "  ,
memory:   new   Memory  ({
options: {
observationalMemory:   true
}
}),
signals: [
new   RingSignals  ({
events: {
button_press: { priority:   "  high  "  , summary:   "  Doorbell was pressed.  "   },
motion_detected: {
priority:   "  medium  "  ,
summary:   "  Motion was detected at the front door.  "  ,
attributes  : (event)   =>   ({
...  event.data.attributes,
location:   "  front door  "
})
},
device_offline: {
priority:   "  low  "  ,
summary:   "  Device went offline.  "  ,
coalesce:   true
}
},
tools: {
clip: { visibleToModel:   true  , durationMs:   15_000   }
}
})
]
});
```


- ` observationalMemory: true` : Turns on observational memory.
- ` events` : Sets the priority and summary for each Ring event.
- ` attributes` : Adds attributes the agent sees on the notification.
- ` coalesce: true` : Merges repeats of an event into one notification.
- ` visibleToModel: true` : Passes the clip to the model. (Requires a model that accepts video input).
- ` durationMs` : Sets the clip length in milliseconds.


Register the agent and a storage adapter on your Mastra instance:


src/mastra/index.ts


```text
import   {   Mastra   }   from   "  @mastra/core  "  ;
import   {   LibSQLStore   }   from   "  @mastra/libsql  "  ;
import   {   homeSecurityAgent   }   from   "  ./agents/home-security-agent  "  ;


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
agents: { homeSecurityAgent },
storage:   new   LibSQLStore  ({
id:   "  mastra-storage  "  ,
url:   "  file:./mastra.db  "
})
});
```


## Multi-player alerts


By default,` RingSignals` sends events to its own thread, but you can also configure` RingSignals` to subscribe to an existing channel thread using the` target` property.


### Channels


This example uses[iMessage](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-imessage-support) . The channel and` RingSignals` share the same` resourceId` and` threadId` , so Ring events arrive in the iMessage conversation.


Install the iMessage adapter:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @photon-ai/chat-adapter-imessage
```


Point the iMessage channel and` RingSignals` at the same thread:


src/mastra/agents/home-security-agent.ts


```text
import   {   Agent   }   from   "  @mastra/core/agent  "  ;
import   {   Memory   }   from   "  @mastra/memory  "  ;
import   {   RingSignals   }   from   "  @mastra/ring-signals  "  ;
import   {   createiMessageAdapter   }   from   "  @photon-ai/chat-adapter-imessage  "  ;


const   resourceId   =   "  home-security-agent  "  ;
const   threadId   =   "  ring-alerts  "  ;


export   const   homeSecurityAgent   =   new   Agent  ({
// ...
id: resourceId,
channels: {
resolveResourceId  : ()   =>   resourceId,
resolveThreadId  : ()   =>   threadId,
adapters: {
imessage: { adapter:   createiMessageAdapter  () }
}
},
signals: [
new   RingSignals  ({
target: { resourceId, threadId }
})
]
});
```


### Mastra client SDK


A web app can also follow the same thread using[subscribeToThread()](https://mastra.ai/reference/client-js/agents#subscribetothread) from the Mastra client SDK:


Terminal


```text
npm   install   @mastra/client-js
```


Subscribe to the thread with the same` resourceId` and` threadId` used by the channel and` RingSignals` :


src/app/subscribe.ts


```text
import   {   MastraClient   }   from   "  @mastra/client-js  "  ;


const   client   =   new   MastraClient  ({   baseUrl:   "  http://localhost:4111  "   });


const   subscription   =   await   client.  getAgent  (  "  home-security-agent  "  ).  subscribeToThread  ({
resourceId:   "  home-security-agent  "  ,
threadId:   "  ring-alerts  "
});


await   subscription.  processDataStream  ({
onChunk  : (chunk)   =>   {
console.  log  (chunk.type, chunk);
}
});
```


